<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>Tabler UI Docs</title>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag "tabler_ui_all" %>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag "tabler_ui/docs" %>
    <%# importmap-rails is not a dependency of this gem (see
        TablerUi::Engine's own 'tabler_ui.importmap' initializer, guarded
        the same way) -- this layout is the only layout left once a host
        mounts this engine (see docs/lib/tabler_ui/docs/engine.rb's own
        header comment), so it has to be the one place that boots JS,
        but only for a host that actually has importmap-rails installed.
        A host that doesn't (e.g. this gem's own Combustion spec harness)
        simply gets no script tags, same as before. %>
    <% if respond_to?(:javascript_importmap_tags) %>
      <%= javascript_importmap_tags %>
    <% end %>
  </head>
  <body>
    <%# Not a full Tabler .page shell with its own <body>-level chrome --
        this engine may be mounted inside a host app that already owns
        page-wide layout/CSS (see this task's own notes on not assuming
        body/global CSS ownership). The navbar + breadcrumb bar +
        page-wrapper/sidebar/content/contents grid below are self-contained
        Tabler markup that renders correctly whether this <body> is the
        whole page or not.

        The breadcrumb bar is a content_for(:breadcrumb) slot, not
        rendered unconditionally here -- only components/show.html.erb
        currently fills it (see its own comment), so the index and forms
        harness pages render with no breadcrumb bar at all rather than an
        empty strip. content_for(:breadcrumb) is guaranteed populated by
        the time this layout runs (Rails renders the child template --
        which sets it -- before wrapping it in the layout). Same guarantee,
        same reasoning, for content_for(:contents) below (see
        components/show.html.erb's own comment on when it does and does
        not call content_for(:contents)). %>
    <% search_and_brand = capture do %>
      <%= link_to "Tabler UI Docs", root_path %>
      <%= render "tabler_ui/docs/shared/search" %>
    <% end %>

    <%# The navbar and the (page-dependent) breadcrumb bar used to be two
        separate elements -- only the breadcrumb bar was `sticky-top`, and
        it lost its own stickiness as soon as the navbar above it (not
        sticky at all) scrolled out of view, since a sticky element sticks
        relative to its OWN normal-flow position, not to whatever else is
        on screen. Wrapping both in one `position: fixed` header fixes
        that: reusing Bootstrap's own `.fixed-top` utility (position:
        fixed; inset-x: 0; top: 0; z-index: 1030) rather than hand-rolling
        the same three declarations in docs.css.

        A fixed header is out of normal flow, so everything below it needs
        an explicit offset -- and that offset has two possible values,
        since the breadcrumb bar (and therefore the header's height) is
        only present on component pages. --docs-header-height holds
        whichever one applies to the current page, set once here on
        .docs-shell (a class this engine's own markup applies, never
        `body` or another page-wide selector -- see docs.css's own header
        comment) so every piece of layout that needs to sit clear of the
        fixed header can read the SAME value: .docs-shell's own
        padding-top (offsets the page-wrapper below), .docs-sidebar and
        .docs-contents' sticky `top` (docs.css), and .docs-content [id]'s
        scroll-margin-top (so clicking an anchor link doesn't land its
        target underneath the fixed header).

        The two values below are the real rendered heights of the navbar
        (4rem) and the breadcrumb bar (2.3125rem) -- both fixed by
        Tabler's own padding tokens, not by their content, so a static
        number is safe here. Re-measure both if either bar's padding ever
        changes. %>
    <% header_height = content_for?(:breadcrumb) ? "6.3125rem" : "4rem" %>

    <%# The design editor (docs/app/views/tabler_ui/docs/editor/show.html.erb)
        needs the full window width for its own three-pane toolbar layout, so
        it flags itself with content_for(:wide_layout) -- the same
        content_for? convention this layout already uses for :breadcrumb
        above and :contents below, rather than an instance variable a
        controller would have to set, or branching on the request path. In
        wide mode the outer container drops its max-width (container-fluid
        instead of container-xl) and the permanently-visible sidebar column
        further down becomes a collapsed-by-default collapse pane spanning
        the full row instead of a standing column, so every column-inch is
        available to the page below. Every other docs page leaves
        content_for(:wide_layout) unset, so this is false there and both
        branches below fall through to the layout's original markup,
        unchanged. %>
    <% wide_layout = content_for?(:wide_layout) %>

    <div class="docs-shell" style="--docs-header-height: <%= header_height %>;">
      <header class="docs-fixed-header fixed-top">
        <%= tabler_ui.navbar(brand: search_and_brand) do |navbar| %>
          <% navbar.right do |nav| %>
            <% nav.dark_mode_toggle %>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>

        <% if content_for?(:breadcrumb) %>
          <div class="docs-breadcrumb-bar bg-body border-bottom">
            <div class="container-xl py-2">
              <%= yield :breadcrumb %>
            </div>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </header>

      <div class="page-wrapper">
        <div class="page-body">
          <div class="<%= wide_layout ? "container-fluid" : "container-xl" %>">
            <div class="row">
              <% if wide_layout %>
                <%# Same collapse markup shared/_sidebar_category.html.erb uses for
                    each category pane inside the sidebar itself -- data-bs-toggle
                    ="collapse" / aria-expanded / aria-controls on the trigger,
                    class="collapse" (no "show": this pane starts collapsed, unlike
                    a category's default-open state) + data-controller=
                    "tabler-ui--collapse" on the pane -- so the chevron rotation
                    docs.css already defines for .docs-sidebar-toggle applies here
                    for free, and there's no second collapse implementation to keep
                    in sync with the first (rule 6). Spans the full row (col-12) so
                    the toggle and, once opened, the sidebar itself, both read as a
                    strip across the top of the page rather than a narrow column. %>
                <div class="col-12 mb-3">
                  <button type="button"
                          class="docs-sidebar-toggle btn btn-link d-flex align-items-center gap-2 px-0 py-1 text-secondary text-uppercase fs-6 text-decoration-none collapsed"
                          data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                          data-bs-target="#docs-wide-sidebar"
                          aria-expanded="false"
                          aria-controls="docs-wide-sidebar">
                    Browse components
                    <%= tabler_ui.icon icon: "chevron-down", size: "sm", html: { class: "docs-sidebar-toggle-icon" } %>
                  </button>
                  <div id="docs-wide-sidebar" class="collapse" data-controller="tabler-ui--collapse">
                    <%= render "tabler_ui/docs/shared/sidebar", current: @nav_current %>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="col-12 mb-4 docs-content">
                  <%= yield %>
                </div>
              <% else %>
                <div class="col-12 col-lg-2 mb-4">
                  <%= render "tabler_ui/docs/shared/sidebar", current: @nav_current %>
                </div>
                <%# Contents (docs/app/views/tabler_ui/docs/shared/_contents.html.erb)
                    only takes up a column when there's one to render -- see
                    components/show.html.erb's own comment on when it calls
                    content_for(:contents). Content widens to fill the space
                    it would have used otherwise, same as the sidebar/content
                    split already did for the breadcrumb bar above. %>
                <div class="col-12 <%= content_for?(:contents) ? "col-lg-8" : "col-lg-10" %> mb-4 docs-content">
                  <%= yield %>
                </div>
                <% if content_for?(:contents) %>
                  <%# Hidden below the lg breakpoint here, on the column, not
                      inside the partial -- there's no room for a third nav
                      column on a narrow viewport, and everything it links to
                      is already in reading order right above it. %>
                  <div class="col-lg-2 mb-4 d-none d-lg-block">
                    <%= yield :contents %>
                  </div>
                <% end %>
              <% end %>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
